@*
    Transcribe.

    THE APP COULD ALWAYS DO THIS AND NEVER OFFERED IT. DictateAsync has been on
    IConversation the whole time - speech in, text out, nothing sent to the
    answering model - with no screen, no menu entry and no way to ask for it. An
    assistant that answers questions but cannot take down a meeting is missing
    the half people actually reach for a recorder to do.

    ONE SESSION, NOT A LOOP OF UTTERANCES. This looped DictateAsync, one call per
    utterance, which worked and does not scale to a meeting: it opened and closed
    the microphone once per sentence, flickering the recording indicator, paying
    the open cost at every pause, and losing whatever was said in the gap between
    closing and reopening. It kept no audio either, so there was nothing to read
    back at the end.

    SessionAsync holds one microphone for the whole meeting, cuts at the silences
    between sentences, appends - and when it stops, puts the entire recording
    through in one pass and replaces the pieced-together text.

    MEASURED, not assumed. A scripted meeting played through a laptop speaker
    into a P30 on 2026-09-07 came back word perfect across four turns, proper
    nouns and figures included - and the closing pass recovered about twelve
    characters the live pieces had dropped, which is the argument for keeping the
    audio made in one number.

    NOTHING IS ANSWERED. The circle elsewhere asks and replies; here it only
    writes down. That is the whole distinction the mode exists to make, and it
    is why this is a mode rather than a button on Chat.
*@
@page "/transcribe"
@inject IConversation Talk
@inject ISpokenLanguage Spoken
@inject VoiceMark Mark
@implements IDisposable

<div class="transcribe">

    @if (_lines.Count == 0 && !_running)
    {
        @*
            The empty state says what it will DO, not what it is. "Transcribe"
            is already on the strip above; repeating it here would spend the
            screen's one chance to explain on a word somebody has just read.
        *@
        <div class="empty">
            <div class="empty-line">Take down what is said</div>
            <div class="empty-sub">
                A meeting, an interview, a clinic appointment. It stays on this
                phone and nothing is sent anywhere.
            </div>
        </div>
    }
    else
    {
        <div class="lines" aria-live="polite">
            @foreach (var line in _lines)
            {
                <p class="line">@line</p>
            }

            @* WHAT IT IS HEARING RIGHT NOW, before the piece ends. Without it a
               long sentence looks like a stall - the same complaint the setup
               screen's frozen countdown earned.

               ONLY WHILE IT IS RUNNING, and that guard is load-bearing.
               Progress<T> hands its reports to the renderer's dispatcher rather
               than running them inline, so the session's LAST report - the whole
               transcript, after the closing pass - can arrive AFTER the code that
               cleared this and moved the text into the lines. The meeting then
               renders twice, the second copy worded slightly differently, which
               reads as the app having lost track of what was said. A partial is
               by definition in progress, so saying so in the condition fixes it
               at the meaning rather than by racing the dispatcher. *@
            @if (_running && _partial is { Length: > 0 })
            {
                <p class="line line-partial">@_partial</p>
            }
        </div>
    }

    @*
        PROGRESS AND REFUSALS ARE NOT THE SAME LINE.
        Both arrive on TurnState.Detail, so "Reading it back…" - which is the app
        working, and the explanation for a pause somebody would otherwise read as
        a hang - rendered in the warning slot, in warning colours, and stayed
        there after the meeting finished. A transcript that ends with what looks
        like an error is a transcript nobody trusts.
        The phase says which it is: Thinking is the app working, Idle with a
        detail is the app refusing.
    *@
    @if (_status is { Length: > 0 })
    {
        <p class="status">@_status</p>
    }

    @if (_trouble is { Length: > 0 })
    {
        <p class="warn">@_trouble</p>
    }

    <div class="controls">
        <button class="record @(_running ? "record-on" : null)" @onclick="Toggle">
            @(_running ? "Stop" : _lines.Count == 0 ? "Start" : "Carry on")
        </button>

        @if (_lines.Count > 0 && !_running)
        {
            <button class="clear" @onclick="Clear">Clear</button>
        }
    </div>

    @if (_lines.Count > 0)
    {
        <p class="count">@_lines.Count line@(_lines.Count == 1 ? "" : "s")</p>
    }
</div>

@code {
    readonly List<string> _lines = [];

    /// <summary>What is being heard right now, before the utterance closes.</summary>
    string? _partial;

    string? _trouble;

    /// <summary>The app working, as opposed to the app refusing.</summary>
    string? _status;
    bool _running;

    CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    /// <summary>Start listening, or stop.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// STOP HAS TO WORK MID-UTTERANCE. A person ending a meeting presses stop
    /// while somebody is still talking, and a button that waits for the current
    /// sentence to finish before responding reads as broken.
    /// </remarks>
    async Task Toggle()
    {
        if (_running)
        {
            _cts?.Cancel();
            return;
        }

        _trouble = null;
        _running = true;
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();

        try
        {
            await ListenAsync(_cts.Token);
        }
        finally
        {
            _running = false;
            _partial = null;
            Mark.Clear();
            _cts?.Dispose();
            _cts = null;
            StateHasChanged();
        }
    }

    /// <summary>One session, held open for the whole meeting.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// THIS USED TO LOOP DictateAsync, one call per utterance. That worked and it
    /// does not scale to the thing the screen is for: it opened and closed the
    /// microphone once per sentence - flickering the recording indicator, paying
    /// the open cost at every pause, and losing whatever was said in the gap
    /// between closing and reopening - and it kept no audio, so there was nothing
    /// to read back at the end.
    /// <para>
    /// A session holds the microphone once, cuts at the silences, and appends. It
    /// reports the transcript so far as each piece lands, and once more when it
    /// has re-read the whole recording.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// FIVE SECONDS, DELIBERATELY. This is a meeting: people pause to think, and
    /// cutting at every breath would shred one sentence across three decodes and
    /// three chances to punctuate it wrongly. A question would want about one
    /// second, which is why the screen chooses rather than taking a default.
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    async Task ListenAsync(CancellationToken ct)
    {
        var updates = new Progress<TurnState>(t =>
        {
            Mark.Report(t);

            // Everything heard so far, replacing what is on screen rather than
            // adding to it: the session owns the transcript and re-reading the
            // recording at the end REPLACES it, so a screen that appended would
            // show the meeting twice.
            if (t.Heard is { Length: > 0 } heard) _partial = heard;

            // WHICH LINE IT GOES ON IS DECIDED BY THE PHASE. Thinking is the app
            // working and belongs in the quiet status line; Idle with a detail is
            // the app refusing, and belongs in the warning.
            if (t.Detail is { Length: > 0 } detail)
            {
                if (t.Phase == TurnPhase.Thinking) _status = detail;
                else _trouble = detail;
            }

            InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);
        });

        try
        {
            var whole = await Talk.SessionAsync(updates, ct, Spoken.Current, silenceMs: 5000);

            _partial = null;
            _status = null;
            if (whole is { Length: > 0 })
            {
                _lines.Clear();
                _lines.Add(whole.Trim());
            }
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException)
        {
            // The ordinary way a recording ends. Whatever the session managed to
            // read back is already on screen.
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            // THE LINES SO FAR ARE KEPT. Losing an hour of a meeting because the
            // closing pass threw would be the worst possible answer.
            _trouble = $"Stopped: {ex.GetType().Name}: {ex.Message}";
        }
    }

    void Clear()
    {
        _lines.Clear();
        _partial = null;
        _trouble = null;
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        _cts?.Cancel();
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }
}
